      body: 'This is a parable, and its people are invented. Esperanza Ruiz ran a bakery in San Antonio, Texas, for twenty-six years. She knew the sourdough schedule by heart, the flour supplier’s cell number, the payroll dates, and which oven ran hot. Nobody else did. One February she slipped on ice in the parking lot and broke her wrist, and for nine days the bakery sold only cookies. Her nephew Mateo sat with her in the clinic and read her a verse from the Bible on his phone: "And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also." (2 Timothy 2:2) Esperanza laughed and said Paul must have run a bakery. That spring she bought a green binder. Every Tuesday after closing she wrote one page and then taught it to Mateo at the bench, and he taught it back to her. By June the binder was full. In August her granddaughter graduated in Phoenix, and Esperanza went for a whole week. The bakery ran. The sourdough rose. Mateo even called the flour supplier himself. When she came home, she found a new page in the binder in Mateo’s handwriting, the one he had started for the next person he would train.',
